<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">22325420</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2012</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>30</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2017</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>12</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">0392-856X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>30</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2012</Year><Season>Mar-Apr</Season></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Clinical and experimental rheumatology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Clin Exp Rheumatol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A novel modality of BAFF-specific inhibitor AMG623 peptibody reduces B-cell number and improves outcomes in murine models of autoimmune disease.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>197</StartPage><EndPage>201</EndPage><MedlinePgn>197-201</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">AMG623, also known as A-623, is an antagonist of B-cell activating factor (BAFF). The present study was to evaluate the effects of AMG623 on murine models of autoimmune diseases.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">AMG623 was generated through phage library. Inhibitory activities of AMG623 against human and murine BAFF were measured by biacore binding and BAFF-mediated B-cell proliferation assay. Pharmacological effects of AMG623 were studied in BALB/c mice, collagen-induced arthritis model (CIA) and in the NZBxNZW F1 lupus model.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">AMG623 binds to both soluble and cell surface BAFF. AMG623 blocks both human murine BAFF binding to the receptors. Treatment of AMG623 resulted in B-cell number reduction, and improvement of arthritis and lupus development in mice.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">AMG623 is a novel modality of BAFF antagonist. AMG623 is a potential therapeutic agent for the treatment of SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, and other B-cell-mediated autoimmune diseases.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hsu</LastName><ForeName>Hailing</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Inflammation, Amgen Inc., Thousand Oaks, CA 91320, USA. hhsu@amgen.com</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Khare</LastName><ForeName>Sanjay D</ForeName><Initials>SD</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lee</LastName><ForeName>Frances</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Miner</LastName><ForeName>Kent</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hu</LastName><ForeName>Yi-Ling</ForeName><Initials>YL</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Stolina</LastName><ForeName>Marina</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hawkins</LastName><ForeName>Nessa</ForeName><Initials>N</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Qing</ForeName><Initials>Q</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ho</LastName><ForeName>Shu-Yin Joanne</ForeName><Initials>SY</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Min</LastName><ForeName>Hosung</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xiong</LastName><ForeName>Fei</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Boone</LastName><ForeName>Tom</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zack</LastName><ForeName>Debra J</ForeName><Initials>DJ</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2012</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>13</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Italy</Country><MedlineTA>Clin Exp Rheumatol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8308521</NlmUniqueID><ISS